00:05:35.951 --> 00:05:40.910
So they just check very simple
syntactic rules on JavaScript

00:05:40.911 --> 00:05:44.130
and try to find some potential
errors in JavaScript code.

00:05:44.131 --> 00:05:47.110
The limitation of static
analysis is pretty obvious.

00:05:47.111 --> 00:05:47.810
They are trying to

00:05:47.811 --> 00:05:50.450
approximate the wrong type
of behavior of a program.

00:05:50.451 --> 00:05:54.764
And because of see limitations
of setting alias analysis or

00:05:54.765 --> 00:05:55.885
other things,

00:05:55.886 --> 00:06:00.211
it's very hard to do precise
step analysis of JavaScript.

00:06:00.212 --> 00:06:02.920
And because of JavaScript
have lots of dynamic

00:06:02.921 --> 00:06:05.005
features like prototypes, eval,

00:06:05.006 --> 00:06:09.130
it's even harder to do stating
analysis for JavaScript.

00:06:09.131 --> 00:06:13.475
So also often times those two
favorite precision of sounding

00:06:13.476 --> 00:06:17.670
by suppressing warnings, and
so that's the challenge.

00:06:17.671 --> 00:06:22.000
And we try to alleviate
this kind of problem

00:06:22.001 --> 00:06:24.550
by using Dynamic Analysis.

00:06:24.551 --> 00:06:28.450
And in the past four year
I've been working on about

00:06:28.451 --> 00:06:31.040
two dynamic analyses
frameworks for JavaScript.

00:06:31.041 --> 00:06:35.750
One's called Jalangi, the other
ones is called platform.

00:06:35.751 --> 00:06:40.041
So for Jalangi, the idea is that
whenever you ask it a piece of

00:06:40.042 --> 00:06:44.757
JavaScript code, Jalangi will
monitor the run-time events from

00:06:44.758 --> 00:06:46.370
the JavaScript code.

00:06:46.371 --> 00:06:50.007
And notify some basic run-time
events like reading a variable

00:06:50.008 --> 00:06:53.369
or calling a function or setting
a property of an object and

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:54.410
stuff like that.

00:06:54.411 --> 00:06:57.452
By overwriting those APIs
[INAUDIBLE] all sorts of

00:06:57.453 --> 00:07:00.493
runtime events of JavaScripts
and then you can do

00:07:00.494 --> 00:07:04.293
some kind of interesting dynamic
analysis on JavaScripts.

00:07:04.294 --> 00:07:08.017
Async-Track and NodeSecurity or
send bugs focuses more on

00:07:08.018 --> 00:07:11.144
the interactions between
the JavaScript code and

00:07:11.145 --> 00:07:13.920
the underlying operating system.

00:07:13.921 --> 00:07:16.428
So whenever there's
an interaction between Java code

00:07:16.429 --> 00:07:18.780
and and
the underlying operating system.

00:07:18.781 --> 00:07:21.922
This framework will actually
notify you some kind

00:07:21.923 --> 00:07:25.736
of event like building a Node.js
module is being required,

00:07:25.737 --> 00:07:28.600
or a synchronous system
API is being called.

00:07:28.601 --> 00:07:30.983
Or a callback is
being registered, or

00:07:30.984 --> 00:07:33.995
a callback is being logged and
stuff like that.

00:07:33.996 --> 00:07:39.448
So I'll introduce the first
part which is Jalangi.

00:07:39.449 --> 00:07:45.930
Jalangi is essentially a source
to source translation framework.

00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:50.774
So for example, if we have
original JavaScript programs

00:07:50.775 --> 00:07:53.595
which look like this which
assigns b's value to a, then

00:07:53.596 --> 00:07:57.580
the simplified instruments the
code in Jalangi looks like this.

00:07:57.581 --> 00:08:00.910
And it's preserving the original
semantics of the Java code.

00:08:00.911 --> 00:08:03.380
Which means they will still
assign this to a, but

00:08:03.381 --> 00:08:06.790
additionally, that's
two more callbacks.

00:08:06.791 --> 00:08:10.352
So one callback corresponds to
the reading variable b another

00:08:10.353 --> 00:08:13.239
callback corresponds to
the writing variable a.

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:15.835
So when you execute
this instrumented code,

00:08:15.836 --> 00:08:18.361
you will not only assign
this value to a, but

00:08:18.362 --> 00:08:21.937
it will additionally call these
two callback functions Yes?

