And we also verify that,
as the node binding layer,

00:43:15.321 --> 00:43:18.310
that all of the communication
are essentially are actually

00:43:18.311 --> 00:43:21.580
being hijacked by our
instantiation code.

00:43:23.420 --> 00:43:26.960
So the key idea of this
instrumentation, is a little bit

00:43:26.961 --> 00:43:29.110
different from the source
to source transpolation.

00:43:30.670 --> 00:43:32.970
So the problem is that,
in JavaScript or

00:43:32.971 --> 00:43:37.130
in Node.js, once you have a
system call, the system call can

00:43:37.131 --> 00:43:39.360
return an object that contains
in another system call.

00:43:40.860 --> 00:43:41.860
And that system call,

00:43:41.861 --> 00:43:44.810
can have an asynchronous
function that takes a callback.

00:43:44.811 --> 00:43:47.180
And whenever callback
is being invoked,

00:43:47.181 --> 00:43:50.810
it may pass in other objects
with other system calls.

00:43:50.811 --> 00:43:54.751
So, it's not just
simply wrapping all of

00:43:54.752 --> 00:43:56.671
the building APIs.

00:43:56.672 --> 00:44:00.331
Because if that API returns an
object that contains other APIs,

00:44:00.332 --> 00:44:02.210
then that may be missed.

00:44:02.211 --> 00:44:04.920
So our approach is that,
we implement our instrumentation

00:44:04.921 --> 00:44:08.140
like a virus in the memory
space of the JavaScript code.

00:44:08.141 --> 00:44:12.170
So whenever there is JavaScript
library objects is being

00:44:12.171 --> 00:44:15.960
required, it's first being
hijacked and being infected or

00:44:15.961 --> 00:44:17.773
wrapped by our
instrumentation framework.

00:44:17.774 --> 00:44:22.337
Then, when that infected system
API, further return here,

00:44:22.338 --> 00:44:26.991
other objects, that's also
being recursively infected, or

00:44:26.992 --> 00:44:28.940
wrapped, by our code.

00:44:28.941 --> 00:44:29.670
So in that case,

00:44:29.671 --> 00:44:32.690
all of the code that's been
descended, that's the children

00:44:32.691 --> 00:44:38.330
of the computing system objects
are all wrapped recursively.

00:44:38.331 --> 00:44:42.020
And when the JavaScript
code tries to use those,

00:44:42.021 --> 00:44:45.530
in fact the objects or
functions to make a system call,

00:44:45.531 --> 00:44:48.290
then they would be monitored
by instrumentation code.

00:44:49.350 --> 00:44:51.120
So that's a key idea.

00:44:51.121 --> 00:44:54.740
But there are also other tricks,
and also other things that we

00:44:54.741 --> 00:44:57.270
need to handle like how to
handle the asynchronous calls,

00:44:57.271 --> 00:44:59.070
and how to handle the call back.

00:44:59.071 --> 00:45:03.090
And those part I won't cover in
more details here because they

00:45:03.091 --> 00:45:07.874
are already covered in the talk.

00:45:07.875 --> 00:45:12.050
So our instrumentation framework
provides these kind of APIs

00:45:12.051 --> 00:45:16.640
a callback is being registered,
a callback is being invoked,

00:45:16.641 --> 00:45:19.200
a synchronous system
call is being called.

00:45:19.201 --> 00:45:21.690
What's their runtime value,
what's their arguments,

00:45:21.691 --> 00:45:24.520
what's their registration
context, and

00:45:24.521 --> 00:45:28.100
what's their callback invocation
context, and stuff like that.

00:45:28.101 --> 00:45:31.120
And over writing those
callbacks you can do a lot of

00:45:31.121 --> 00:45:32.710
other interesting stuff.

00:45:32.711 --> 00:45:37.308
So one we thing look at is
the security issue in Node JS.

00:45:37.309 --> 00:45:41.228
So currently there
are about over

00:45:41.229 --> 00:45:46.548
500,000 npm packages right now,
and

00:45:46.549 --> 00:45:51.590
they're still increasing
very rapidly.

00:45:51.591 --> 00:45:55.850
NPM doesn't actually enforce
any security checking, or

00:45:55.851 --> 00:45:58.720
manual inspection, or
manual review process,

00:45:58.721 --> 00:46:02.000
so anyone can just publish
a malware in NPM, and

00:46:02.001 --> 00:46:03.510
anyone can download
it very easily.

00:46:05.150 --> 00:46:09.700
So There are currently
two companies.

00:46:09.701 --> 00:46:11.130
There are maybe other companies,
but

00:46:11.131 --> 00:46:12.930
there are two major companies.

00:46:12.931 --> 00:46:16.810
One of these is called Node
Security Platform, another one

00:46:16.811 --> 00:46:20.030
is called Snyk.io, that are
dedicated to Node.js Security.

00:46:20.031 --> 00:46:23.570
What they are doing is that they
have a team of security experts,

00:46:23.571 --> 00:46:26.550
You manually inspect
the MPM packages, and

00:46:26.551 --> 00:46:30.160
we also collect all kinds of
vulnerability reports on gethub,

00:46:30.161 --> 00:46:32.510
and we collect
the vulnerabilities and

00:46:32.511 --> 00:46:34.630
compile them into
their database.

00:46:34.631 --> 00:46:39.550
And then they will make it
to check the meta file of

00:46:39.551 --> 00:46:43.530
the MPM package and
say If a package's dependencies,

00:46:43.531 --> 00:46:47.072
and its dependencies'
dependencies,

00:46:47.073 --> 00:46:49.590
are any vulnerable packages
in their database.

00:46:49.591 --> 00:46:53.010
By just matching the packaging
name and their version.

00:46:53.011 --> 00:46:55.960
But we think this
approach is not scalable.

00:46:55.961 --> 00:47:00.110
And so,
we use our node send box to

00:47:00.111 --> 00:47:04.640
Dynamically check about
330,000 npm packages.

00:47:04.641 --> 00:47:09.360
And we found about 292
security vulnerabilities and

00:47:09.361 --> 00:47:12.710
we're still in the process of
reporting those vulnerabilities

00:47:12.711 --> 00:47:17.910
to those companies and so far we
have already 150 confirmed and

00:47:17.911 --> 00:47:19.709
the 120 are considered
highly severe.

00:47:20.910 --> 00:47:24.020
Those two companies collectively

00:47:24.021 --> 00:47:26.380
have about 600 security
vulnerabilities.

00:47:26.381 --> 00:47:28.244
So a quarter of their
vulnerabilities

00:47:28.245 --> 00:47:29.240
are recorded by us.

