00:48:24.651 --> 00:48:26.230
&gt;&gt; So-
&gt;&gt; Why does that make sense?

00:48:26.231 --> 00:48:28.990
&gt;&gt; So in NPM,
it works like this.

00:48:28.991 --> 00:48:31.230
First example,
if you have NPM packages,

00:48:31.231 --> 00:48:33.550
it may depend on
other 20 packages.

00:48:33.551 --> 00:48:36.570
Those other 20 packages
may recursively depend on

00:48:36.571 --> 00:48:37.800
hundreds of other packages.

00:48:37.801 --> 00:48:39.920
So it's actually called
dependency hell.

00:48:39.921 --> 00:48:41.270
When you write a package,

00:48:41.271 --> 00:48:44.572
I think someone did an
investigation about say if you

00:48:44.573 --> 00:48:47.930
implement some
functionalities of publish or

00:48:47.931 --> 00:48:52.230
mpm, only 5% of the code is
written by that developer.

00:48:52.231 --> 00:48:56.320
Other 95% is coming from those
dependencies, and because of

00:48:56.321 --> 00:49:01.180
this dependency hell, any
package may accidentally require

00:49:01.181 --> 00:49:07.400
any other package developed by
the By some malicious users or

00:49:07.401 --> 00:49:10.650
other people who are not very
helpful with their codes.

00:49:10.651 --> 00:49:12.190
They may actually introduce

00:49:12.191 --> 00:49:14.140
vulnerable teams
into their code.

00:49:14.141 --> 00:49:19.510
So it's actually a kind of
a serious issue in NPM.

00:49:19.511 --> 00:49:23.069
This is one of the malicious
packages that we found if you

00:49:23.070 --> 00:49:27.085
just installed the NPM package
you will change your background

00:49:27.086 --> 00:49:30.210
of your computer into
A naked David Hasselhoff.

00:49:31.310 --> 00:49:33.280
And another example for

00:49:33.281 --> 00:49:38.013
security vulnerabilities
is the Directory Traversal.

00:49:38.014 --> 00:49:41.004
So we found about 220
packages having this issue so

00:49:41.005 --> 00:49:44.526
Directory Traversal means that
Whenever Apache tries to set up

00:49:44.527 --> 00:49:47.383
the http server or,
in other words, a server, and

00:49:47.384 --> 00:49:50.654
when the client tries to request
the file from the server.

00:49:50.655 --> 00:49:54.005
If the outreach is not very
careful, he or she may just

00:49:54.006 --> 00:49:57.953
concatenate the requested URL
with the basdir, then just read

00:49:57.954 --> 00:50:01.510
the file and stream it out
to the remote clients.

00:50:01.511 --> 00:50:04.322
That's vulnerable to
this kind of attack,

00:50:04.323 --> 00:50:07.058
because attacker can
just use this URL like

00:50:07.059 --> 00:50:12.310
../../ to request any file or
size of the working directory.

00:50:12.311 --> 00:50:15.020
And there are lots of other
variants of this kind

00:50:15.021 --> 00:50:16.239
of attacking URL, so

00:50:16.240 --> 00:50:19.789
it's a little bit hard to defend
against this kind of things.

00:50:19.790 --> 00:50:20.594
And even worse,

00:50:20.595 --> 00:50:23.759
all of these directory traversal
vulnerabilities can be further

00:50:23.760 --> 00:50:26.359
exploited to perform
denial-of-service attack.

00:50:26.360 --> 00:50:29.598
Or just requesting this
/dev/zero file, which is a Linux

00:50:29.599 --> 00:50:33.140
virtual files that provides
unlimited strings with zeros.

00:50:33.141 --> 00:50:35.128
And if you read that
file on the server,

00:50:35.129 --> 00:50:38.040
then it would just lead to
the consumption of all memory.

00:50:38.041 --> 00:50:42.162
Or leads to a consumption
of the network bandwidth,

00:50:42.163 --> 00:50:47.140
which is essentially causing
a denial-of-service attack.

00:50:48.430 --> 00:50:49.441
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] fixing the work.

00:50:49.442 --> 00:50:52.860
Does your tool able to find
that kind of problems?

00:50:52.861 --> 00:50:53.550
&gt;&gt; So what do you mean by hash?

00:50:53.551 --> 00:50:57.096
&gt;&gt; So basically, they used
to have a constant seed for

00:50:57.097 --> 00:51:00.972
