00:53:01.780 --> 00:53:05.578
And this instrumentation
framework also enables other

00:53:05.579 --> 00:53:07.280
kinds of applications.

00:53:07.281 --> 00:53:10.397
For example, because it can keep
track of asynchronous events in

00:53:10.398 --> 00:53:12.618
Node.js, you can
actually visualize them.

00:53:12.619 --> 00:53:15.686
And it will help debugging, it
will help program understanding,

00:53:15.687 --> 00:53:16.736
and stuff like that.

00:53:16.737 --> 00:53:21.338
It's also helping TTD for better
time travel and debugging.

00:53:21.339 --> 00:53:23.804
Because when you're in
a callback function,

00:53:23.805 --> 00:53:27.029
in the time traveling debugger,
often times you want to jump

00:53:27.030 --> 00:53:30.210
back to the position where
the callbacker was registered.

00:53:30.211 --> 00:53:33.430
Instead of jumping back to the
exact functions that were being

00:53:33.431 --> 00:53:36.040
executed before that callback
function was being executed.

00:53:37.680 --> 00:53:44.850
And it can also be used to
detect security issues, or this.

00:53:44.851 --> 00:53:47.604
So maybe you can also implement
a container inside Node.js

00:53:47.605 --> 00:53:50.414
because you are essentially
hijacking all the interactions

00:53:50.415 --> 00:53:53.630
between Node.js and
the underlying operating system.

00:53:53.631 --> 00:53:57.095
You can implement some sort
of containerization just for

00:53:57.096 --> 00:54:00.000
Node.js.

00:54:00.001 --> 00:54:02.210
Those are just a few examples.

00:54:03.690 --> 00:54:05.520
For the last part
of my presentation,

00:54:05.521 --> 00:54:09.880
I actually want to focus
a little bit more on the project

00:54:09.881 --> 00:54:12.230
that I've been working on
before I started my PhD.

00:54:12.231 --> 00:54:15.403
So I was working on applying
machine-learning techniques to

00:54:15.404 --> 00:54:17.721
some of the software
engineering problems,

00:54:17.722 --> 00:54:19.856
specifically like
fault localization,

00:54:19.857 --> 00:54:22.490
defect prediction,
predicting bug fixing time.

00:54:26.580 --> 00:54:31.024
So, but in summary,
what essentially I was doing is

00:54:31.025 --> 00:54:34.256
actually either
cleaning the data for

00:54:34.257 --> 00:54:38.296
defect prediction or
for fault localization,

00:54:38.297 --> 00:54:42.648
each one as a problem is
like a prediction problem.

00:54:42.649 --> 00:54:44.531
And so what we did is that
you can use measure and

00:54:44.532 --> 00:54:47.055
eliminate the effect of noisy
data and give back prediction.

00:54:47.056 --> 00:54:49.705
Because often times when we
collect some data from the soft

00:54:49.706 --> 00:54:52.519
engineering projects, there may,
because humans make many

00:54:52.520 --> 00:54:56.030
mistakes all the time, and
the collected data may be noisy.

00:54:56.031 --> 00:55:00.970
And we measure the effectiveness
of those, the native effect

00:55:00.971 --> 00:55:06.710
of those noises in those data
sets, to the prediction result.

00:55:06.711 --> 00:55:12.044
And we also worked on
some projects that

00:55:12.045 --> 00:55:17.840
tries to eliminate some noisy
data in those data sets and

00:55:17.841 --> 00:55:21.040
try to improve
the prediction accuracy.

00:55:21.041 --> 00:55:24.228
We also tried to reduce
the redundancy in training data

00:55:24.229 --> 00:55:27.419
while still preserving
the prediction accuracy, and

00:55:27.420 --> 00:55:28.840
stuff like that.

00:55:28.841 --> 00:55:32.340
And because we worked on this
project a little bit earlier,

00:55:32.341 --> 00:55:35.360
back in 2011 and 12, so
we actually did a lot of

00:55:35.361 --> 00:55:38.450
the feature engineering work and
incorporating domain

00:55:38.451 --> 00:55:40.487
specific knowledge
into the training data.

00:55:40.488 --> 00:55:43.135
It's essentially adding more
information into the prediction

00:55:43.136 --> 00:55:46.405
models so that it can have
better prediction results, or

00:55:46.406 --> 00:55:49.525
even changing
the machine-learning process.

00:55:49.526 --> 00:55:51.305
Originally, it's doing
batch processing, and

00:55:51.306 --> 00:55:54.795
then we incorporated some
simple feedback to the loop so

00:55:54.796 --> 00:55:57.475
that they can incorporate
more information for

00:55:57.476 --> 00:55:58.664
better prediction.

00:55:58.665 --> 00:56:03.460
However, I actually don't
want to focus on those parts,

00:56:03.461 --> 00:56:08.330
because there are actually some
of the more important lessons

00:56:08.331 --> 00:56:10.500
that I've learned from doing
this kind of research.

00:56:12.530 --> 00:56:14.950
So to show you one example,

00:56:14.951 --> 00:56:18.875
this is the technical
fault localization.

00:56:18.876 --> 00:56:22.949
So what this is trying to do is,
if you have a program and

00:56:22.950 --> 00:56:24.732
it contains some bugs.

00:56:24.733 --> 00:56:27.739
And you have a set
of test cases, and

00:56:27.740 --> 00:56:32.349
when we run the program on
the set of test cases, some of

00:56:32.350 --> 00:56:37.380
the test cases will pass, some
of the test cases will fail.

00:56:37.381 --> 00:56:39.875
And so the question is can
you automatically learn or

00:56:39.876 --> 00:56:41.747
can you automatically
do something for

00:56:41.748 --> 00:56:45.210
instance find out the bug
locations in that program.

00:56:45.211 --> 00:56:48.810
So to the approaches that we
worked on is called statistic

00:56:48.811 --> 00:56:51.020
bug localization.

00:56:51.021 --> 00:56:54.077
It's actually, first they're
collecting the profiling

00:56:54.078 --> 00:56:57.010
information, like coverage
information for each one of

00:56:57.011 --> 00:57:00.490
the statement, in each test
coverage, in each test cases.

00:57:00.491 --> 00:57:03.319
Then we also gather the test
outcome as a letter,

00:57:03.320 --> 00:57:04.430
put it right here.

00:57:04.431 --> 00:57:09.082
And then we use some techniques
to try to predict, or infer,

00:57:09.083 --> 00:57:12.743
the probable bug location
inside the program.

00:57:12.744 --> 00:57:16.400
And actually back in 2009,
people in that

00:57:16.401 --> 00:57:21.184
research area already started
using [INAUDIBLE] to predict so

00:57:21.185 --> 00:57:25.330
where is the bug,
is probably inside the program.

00:57:25.331 --> 00:57:30.313
So the first use of to, based
on this coverage information and

00:57:30.314 --> 00:57:33.016
this letter,
which is pass the test or

00:57:33.017 --> 00:57:36.155
fail to try to predict
the testing outcome.

00:57:36.156 --> 00:57:39.148
And when they learn
that works and

00:57:39.149 --> 00:57:41.970
they just do some
feature selection and

00:57:41.971 --> 00:57:46.331
see which statements are the
most influential factor to make

00:57:46.332 --> 00:57:50.366
the predictor predict that
this output is fail or pass.

00:57:50.367 --> 00:57:51.150
Yes?

00:57:52.490 --> 00:57:55.137
&gt;&gt; Is it an assumption that
there is only one bug?

00:57:55.138 --> 00:57:59.436
&gt;&gt; No, there are two different
kinds of prediction techniques.

00:57:59.437 --> 00:58:00.698
One is called single bug.

00:58:00.699 --> 00:58:01.943
Bug localization and

00:58:01.944 --> 00:58:05.550
another one just assumes there
are multiple bugs in the code.

00:58:05.551 --> 00:58:08.526
&gt;&gt; And where does that
training data come from for

00:58:08.527 --> 00:58:10.215
training these systems?

00:58:10.216 --> 00:58:13.070
So you have a repository of bug
fixes in the past that you had?

00:58:13.071 --> 00:58:14.620
&gt;&gt; No, no bug fixes.

00:58:14.621 --> 00:58:18.752
So let me see.

00:58:18.753 --> 00:58:22.960
So in that example
that I just mentioned,

00:58:22.961 --> 00:58:27.780
the training data, so
it just looks like this.

00:58:27.781 --> 00:58:30.620
Each instance is
the current information and

00:58:30.621 --> 00:58:34.810
each labor of that instance
is just the test outcome.

00:58:34.811 --> 00:58:38.610
And then, they just ask
the machine learning detector to

00:58:38.611 --> 00:58:42.230
first predict accurately
the test outcome.

00:58:42.231 --> 00:58:45.940
Then it tries to take a look at
the machine learning model and

00:58:45.941 --> 00:58:49.290
try to find out which
statement or which feature

00:58:49.291 --> 00:58:53.660
is the most influential effect
leading to make such a predictor

00:58:53.661 --> 00:58:56.185
to believe that this should
be a failed test case.

00:58:57.225 --> 00:59:02.285
Yeah, so but
a lot of people have take a look

00:59:02.286 --> 00:59:04.495
at a lot of different kinds of
machine learning techniques.

00:59:04.496 --> 00:59:06.995
They also even use decision
tree, which is even easier for

00:59:06.996 --> 00:59:09.955
you to find out which line of
code is most influential for

00:59:11.705 --> 00:59:14.531
predicting and test the outcome. Yes?

00:59:14.532 --> 00:59:16.860
&gt;&gt; So this side [INAUDIBLE].

00:59:16.861 --> 00:59:20.108
So you mention that though you
can look at the model to see

00:59:20.109 --> 00:59:23.221
which part of the model is
making that prediction that

00:59:23.222 --> 00:59:25.110
the test case will pass or fail.

00:59:25.111 --> 00:59:29.292
But how would you do this for
let's say neural networks where

00:59:29.293 --> 00:59:33.228
the models might be quite
intricate, maybe not as simple

00:59:33.229 --> 00:59:35.766
as the [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Yeah I think in that paper

00:59:35.767 --> 00:59:37.680
they have some heuristics, too,

00:59:37.681 --> 00:59:41.244
but I don't remember actually
the technical details because we

00:59:41.245 --> 00:59:43.950
actually don't work on
that kind of technique.

00:59:43.951 --> 00:59:47.820
Because later on I
will actually show you

00:59:47.821 --> 00:59:50.570
that kind of machine learning
techniques is not very

00:59:51.600 --> 00:59:54.040
accurate in this
kind of problem.

00:59:54.041 --> 00:59:58.810
And because,
a more simple statistic model is

00:59:58.811 --> 01:00:01.260
actually more effective for
this kind of approach.

01:00:01.261 --> 01:00:03.630
But later on,
I'll actually tell you that

01:00:03.631 --> 01:00:05.280
even this kind of approach
is not practical.

01:00:05.281 --> 01:00:08.580
And I'll show you some of the
lessons I learned why this kind

01:00:08.581 --> 01:00:11.650
of approach is not actually
practical for real world usage.

01:00:13.500 --> 01:00:15.450
So people have
learned over time,

01:00:15.451 --> 01:00:18.820
so this is actually called a
spectrum-based bug localization.

01:00:18.821 --> 01:00:22.796
I think it was a quite hot
research area 10 years ago, and

01:00:22.797 --> 01:00:27.180
I think more than 100 papers
are publishing software sharing

01:00:27.181 --> 01:00:29.840
conferences for
that research area.

01:00:29.841 --> 01:00:36.670
And so, different people propose
different kinds of techniques

01:00:36.671 --> 01:00:40.980
to tackle the suspicious core
of a statement in the program.

01:00:40.981 --> 01:00:43.450
And they try to
rank the statements

01:00:43.451 --> 01:00:46.380
based on their suspiciousness
and then they just

01:00:46.381 --> 01:00:51.150
expect the developer to inspect
the buggy location one by one.

01:00:51.151 --> 01:00:54.665
And hopefully this technique
can help them find out

01:00:54.666 --> 01:00:55.980
the bug location.

01:00:57.390 --> 01:01:01.080
So one of the most effective
techniques is actually

01:01:01.081 --> 01:01:02.530
the simplest one.

01:01:02.531 --> 01:01:04.809
So the key idea is that
they use this formula, for

01:01:04.810 --> 01:01:07.924
each one of the statements, to
calculate this suspicious score,

01:01:07.925 --> 01:01:08.770
independently.

01:01:10.780 --> 01:01:12.706
So this formula is
actually quite easy.

01:01:12.707 --> 01:01:16.740
On the numerator, it's just
the counted number of failure

01:01:16.741 --> 01:01:19.380
traces that are covering
that current statement.

01:01:19.381 --> 01:01:21.919
While on the denominator,
it's just the number of failing

01:01:21.920 --> 01:01:24.670
traces and the number of
which traces are covering S.

01:01:24.671 --> 01:01:29.490
So the basic intuition
is that if a statement

01:01:29.491 --> 01:01:33.220
is covered more in failing test
cases while covered less in

01:01:33.221 --> 01:01:38.000
passing test cases, then it's
more likely to contain the bug.

01:01:38.001 --> 01:01:40.413
Yes, so
it's a very simple idea right?

01:01:40.414 --> 01:01:43.792
And usually on some
small benchmarks, or

01:01:43.793 --> 01:01:48.424
even on some medium programs
it often ranks the actual bug

01:01:48.425 --> 01:01:52.379
location, I think the top
1% of the rank list,

01:01:52.380 --> 01:01:56.935
with respect to all of these
statements in the program.

01:01:56.936 --> 01:02:00.310
And I also have a demo for that.

01:02:01.500 --> 01:02:03.860
It's actually based
on Jalangi as well.

01:02:03.861 --> 01:02:06.947
So because our Jalangi is
instantiation framework you can

01:02:06.948 --> 01:02:08.774
do a lot of things
based on that, so

01:02:08.775 --> 01:02:11.110
I just implemented them
in Jalangi as well.

01:02:11.111 --> 01:02:16.040
And this is actually a simple
application for MOOC.

01:02:16.041 --> 01:02:20.332
Like students can send me
some buggy solutions and

01:02:20.333 --> 01:02:26.080
the instructor can upload
the reference implementation.

01:02:26.081 --> 01:02:28.795
Then our system can just
randomly generate a bunch of

01:02:28.796 --> 01:02:32.067
test cases and then runs on the
referencing implementation and

01:02:32.068 --> 01:02:33.867
gets the corrected test outcome.

01:02:33.868 --> 01:02:36.151
Then we just run those
test cases on the buggy

01:02:36.152 --> 01:02:39.135
implementation, and then we
also get the test outcome.

01:02:39.136 --> 01:02:42.824
And then we just compare their
test outcome to decide whether

01:02:42.825 --> 01:02:46.080
or not test case is passing or
failing.

01:02:46.081 --> 01:02:47.690
And then based on
that simple formula,

01:02:47.691 --> 01:02:50.720
we can calculate
the possible bug location.

01:02:50.721 --> 01:02:55.395
For example, if I just run, then
you will flag all the potential

01:02:55.396 --> 01:02:57.483
bug locations in the codes.

01:02:57.484 --> 01:03:00.003
And if it's more
likely to be a bug,

01:03:00.004 --> 01:03:04.170
then you'll have a darker
background color.

01:03:04.171 --> 01:03:10.470
So here it shows that this is a
bug, it's actually a seeded bug.

01:03:10.471 --> 01:03:14.395
And if I just fix it and
I run again,

01:03:14.396 --> 01:03:19.040
then it will show no more
failing test cases and

01:03:19.041 --> 01:03:21.060
every statement is
considered clean.

01:03:22.540 --> 01:03:24.740
&gt;&gt; So you use a technique that
you had published a long time

01:03:24.741 --> 01:03:26.950
ago for this or?

01:03:26.951 --> 01:03:29.830
&gt;&gt; It's actually not
the techniques that I published

01:03:29.831 --> 01:03:31.360
a long time ago, but

01:03:31.361 --> 01:03:34.460
our technique was trying to
improve this kind of technique.

01:03:34.461 --> 01:03:37.636
So this kind of ranking
technique, say,

01:03:37.637 --> 01:03:41.178
is actually based on
very ancient statistical

01:03:41.179 --> 01:03:43.831
back-localization technique.

01:03:43.832 --> 01:03:46.743
It just subs a very simple
formula that I showed

01:03:46.744 --> 01:03:48.070
right here.

01:03:48.071 --> 01:03:51.280
&gt;&gt; Well hang on a second, so
what is your contribution here?

01:03:51.281 --> 01:03:53.562
&gt;&gt; So
our contribution here is, so

01:03:53.563 --> 01:03:57.194
one project that we did is
try to reduce the number of

01:03:57.195 --> 01:04:01.505
trainings that are needed by
this kind of prediction model,

01:04:01.506 --> 01:04:05.220
while still preserving
its accuracy.

01:04:05.221 --> 01:04:07.270
So in other things that we did,
was that,

01:04:07.271 --> 01:04:11.020
because this technique is just
doing batch processes, right?

01:04:11.021 --> 01:04:14.400
Just to give you the programs
and run all the test cases and

01:04:14.401 --> 01:04:18.170
then need to give you a rank
without actually asking for

01:04:18.171 --> 01:04:20.015
any feedback from
the developers.

01:04:20.016 --> 01:04:21.622
Well, we found that
there is a simple way

01:04:21.623 --> 01:04:22.860
to incorporate the feedback.

01:04:22.861 --> 01:04:26.614
Which means when the developers
are reading these statements

01:04:26.615 --> 01:04:27.984
from top to bottom, so

01:04:27.985 --> 01:04:31.304
sometimes he may be sure that
this statement is clean or

01:04:31.305 --> 01:04:34.000
he may be sure that this
statement is buggy.

01:04:34.001 --> 01:04:37.902
Then we can incorporate that
feedback into the photo edition

01:04:37.903 --> 01:04:41.660
process, and then improve
the photolization accuracy.

01:04:41.661 --> 01:04:44.087
But that is not my
main point here,

01:04:44.088 --> 01:04:51.265
so, That was the recent yeah,

01:04:51.266 --> 01:04:54.967
because this research area was
a very hot research area, but

01:04:54.968 --> 01:04:58.816
sometimes later people have
realized that these techniques,

01:04:58.817 --> 01:05:02.250
it's actually not yet
practical for real world usage.

01:05:02.251 --> 01:05:06.159
So after I actually spent one
year doing research trying to

01:05:06.160 --> 01:05:10.468
improve the performance of this
kind of system on benchmarks for

01:05:10.469 --> 01:05:12.623
medium size application size,

01:05:12.624 --> 01:05:16.487
I finally tried to to make it
work on large applications.

01:05:16.488 --> 01:05:20.020
Like Firefox, which contains
millions of lines of code.

01:05:20.021 --> 01:05:23.858
Then I realized this technique
is actually not practical yet.

01:05:23.859 --> 01:05:27.785
Because for one of the reasoning
is that when you are applying

01:05:27.786 --> 01:05:31.712
this techniques to Firefox,
if there are 1 million lines of

01:05:31.713 --> 01:05:35.177
code, and if the buggy code
is ranked in the top 1%,

01:05:35.178 --> 01:05:38.334
then then the developer
still has to inspect tens

01:05:38.335 --> 01:05:43.400
of thousands of lines of code,
which is actually not practical.

01:05:43.401 --> 01:05:45.840
And also people have
learned over time or

01:05:45.841 --> 01:05:50.240
either do an empirical study
to find out that oftentimes,

01:05:50.241 --> 01:05:53.540
when you show a buggy line to
a developer without giving them

01:05:53.541 --> 01:05:58.846
any logical explanation,
then they just get confused.

01:05:58.847 --> 01:06:02.494
And then they may just miss
that bug, even if you actually

01:06:02.495 --> 01:06:06.449
always show the bug in the top
result, in the ranking result.

01:06:06.450 --> 01:06:11.422
And another bigger issue that
people have found over time is

01:06:11.423 --> 01:06:17.103
that false positives can lead to
very radical interest drop-off.

01:06:17.104 --> 01:06:21.064
If the developer inspects
the top five results and

01:06:21.065 --> 01:06:24.183
They still can't find
any of the bugs.

01:06:24.184 --> 01:06:26.298
And then we just fall back to
the old way of doing things,

01:06:26.299 --> 01:06:27.425
like setting a breakpoint and

01:06:27.426 --> 01:06:28.970
do manual debugging,
stuff like that.

01:06:28.971 --> 01:06:32.368
And let's just generalize
a little bit to choose some of

01:06:32.369 --> 01:06:35.840
the challenges for applying
machine learning to program

01:06:35.841 --> 01:06:38.750
languages often during problems.

01:06:38.751 --> 01:06:43.540
And so
one of the bigger challenges for

01:06:43.541 --> 01:06:47.550
that area is that usually when
you try to use machine learning

01:06:47.551 --> 01:06:53.120
to predict something in a
software engineering project, so

01:06:53.121 --> 01:06:56.270
usually this problem actually
requires very high accuracy.

01:06:56.271 --> 01:07:01.130
Because in that area there
are often an alternative exists.

01:07:01.131 --> 01:07:04.013
Like for the photo audition, a
developer can always use manual

01:07:04.014 --> 01:07:06.900
debugging or there are always
other kinds of things that they

01:07:06.901 --> 01:07:09.610
can do to find the bug.

01:07:09.611 --> 01:07:13.341
And if the accuracy is not high
enough, then people would just

01:07:13.342 --> 01:07:16.231
get boring or
they will just get disappointed.

01:07:16.232 --> 01:07:19.396
Then they will just fall back to
their old way of doing things.

01:07:19.397 --> 01:07:23.852
And also, on this kind of, if we
try to use machine learning to

01:07:23.853 --> 01:07:27.541
generate something from,
or software during, or

01:07:27.542 --> 01:07:32.030
predict something for
the software during project.

01:07:32.031 --> 01:07:34.240
Usually, it's actually
project-specific,

01:07:34.241 --> 01:07:37.130
like I want to predict something
just for that project.

01:07:37.131 --> 01:07:38.825
Or unless that's a very large,

01:07:38.826 --> 01:07:41.340
open-source project, or that's
a very large commercial project

01:07:41.341 --> 01:07:44.540
that involves a lot of the human
interactions, there won't be

01:07:44.541 --> 01:07:47.720
a lot of labor data available
for machine learning.

01:07:49.750 --> 01:07:51.510
So that's another bigger issue.

01:07:51.511 --> 01:07:56.409
So another issue is that
unless the machine learning

01:07:56.410 --> 01:08:01.641
model is fully automated and
its accuracy is very high,

01:08:01.642 --> 01:08:07.454
the entire process always have
to involve humans in the loop.

01:08:07.455 --> 01:08:11.481
Which means that when it
generates some results, it has

01:08:11.482 --> 01:08:16.021
to ask the humans to inspect the
result, and then ask the humans

01:08:16.022 --> 01:08:20.188
to incorporate the results
into the real world product.

01:08:20.189 --> 01:08:25.150
And so if the machine learning
project can not give like logic

01:08:25.151 --> 01:08:29.340
explanation or make
the results understandable to

01:08:29.341 --> 01:08:34.300
the developers,
then may be very hard to

01:08:34.301 --> 01:08:38.911
apply to the kind of technology
like to real world production.

01:08:40.930 --> 01:08:45.018
So maintain ability is like also
another bigger issue even for

01:08:45.019 --> 01:08:47.070
program synthesis I believe.

01:08:47.071 --> 01:08:52.710
If you generate a piece of code
and if it's too hard to read and

01:08:52.711 --> 01:08:54.116
developer can not
understand that,

01:08:54.117 --> 01:08:56.325
when there's a bug it
will be very hard to fix.

01:08:56.326 --> 01:09:01.103
So this is my last
slide summarizing

01:09:01.104 --> 01:09:05.252
some of the future interests.

01:09:05.253 --> 01:09:07.798
So, I'm mostly interested
in users working on

01:09:07.799 --> 01:09:09.590
JavaScript Toolchain Support.

01:09:09.591 --> 01:09:14.013
Or using all kinds of techniques
to improve the reliability,

01:09:14.014 --> 01:09:17.682
performance, and
security of software systems.

01:09:17.683 --> 01:09:19.997
And also software systems
definitely including,

01:09:19.998 --> 01:09:22.440
is definitely including
machine learning systems.

01:09:22.441 --> 01:09:26.316
I'm also very interested in
making this dynamic analysis

01:09:26.317 --> 01:09:29.886
research offering a little
bit more sophisticated.

01:09:29.887 --> 01:09:33.190
Because oftentimes we see a very
high run-time overhead in

01:09:33.191 --> 01:09:35.530
the instrumentation techniques.

01:09:35.531 --> 01:09:41.358
So if we can just integrate this
instrumentation framework for

01:09:41.359 --> 01:09:46.311
directly into the engine,
it will hopefully achieve better

01:09:46.312 --> 01:09:50.900
performance and could reduce
the run-time overhead.

01:09:50.901 --> 01:09:53.214
So another thing that I'm
very interested in is program

01:09:53.215 --> 01:09:53.760
synthesis.

01:09:55.430 --> 01:10:00.584
I guess, program synthesis, so
essentially, in the original

01:10:00.585 --> 01:10:04.980
way, developer are writing
software by themselves.

01:10:04.981 --> 01:10:06.860
That's, in machine
learning people's terms,

01:10:06.861 --> 01:10:08.790
that's like an expert system,
right?

01:10:08.791 --> 01:10:11.180
You give the specification
either in terms of a formal

01:10:11.181 --> 01:10:12.810
specification, or

01:10:12.811 --> 01:10:16.150
you give specification in terms
of input/output examples.

01:10:16.151 --> 01:10:19.350
Then the experts are just
the customers and developers.

01:10:19.351 --> 01:10:22.490
Then based on those
input/outputs,

01:10:22.491 --> 01:10:25.310
then they just develop
expert system that

01:10:25.311 --> 01:10:28.900
use a rule-based system to
map the input to the output.

01:10:28.901 --> 01:10:33.130
But right now it's interesting
to see that the program

01:10:33.131 --> 01:10:38.300
synthesis can just search for
programs and generates output.

01:10:38.301 --> 01:10:41.250
And I'm actually most
interested in speeding up

01:10:41.251 --> 01:10:46.050
the searching process to
reduce the searching time,

01:10:46.051 --> 01:10:49.850
because I feel that scalability
could be a bigger issue for

01:10:49.851 --> 01:10:50.950
program synthesis field.

01:10:52.050 --> 01:10:55.580
Because the larger the program
you want to synthesis or

01:10:55.581 --> 01:10:58.720
more expressive the language
you are trying to use,

01:10:58.721 --> 01:11:01.630
then it seems that the search
space will be much larger.

01:11:01.631 --> 01:11:03.960
So it will be interesting
to use some heuristics or

01:11:03.961 --> 01:11:07.630
some other techniques
to reduce the searching

01:11:07.631 --> 01:11:09.719
space to further
speed up the process.

